<h2>Three cases, three forums</h2>
<p>The NIA's case — RC-21/2025/NIA/DLI2, arising from the Lal Qila blast — stood registered under Sections 103(1), 109(1) and 61(2) of the BNS, Sections 16 and 18 of the UAPA and Sections 3 and 4 of the Explosive Substances Act, pending before the NIA Special Court at Patiala House. Two connected state cases, however, were travelling separately: FIR 162/2025 of P.S. Nowgam, Srinagar, before the ASJ (TADA/POTA) at Srinagar, and FIR 229/2025 of P.S. Dhauj, Faridabad, before the JMIC at Faridabad. The Central Government issued takeover orders under Section 6(5) of the NIA Act in December 2025 and January 2026, and the Union then sought transfer and clubbing from the Supreme Court.</p>

<h2>The Court's reasoning</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Connected offences</strong> — The MHA's orders treated both state FIRs as connected to the NIA case (para 8).</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Overlap in substance</strong> — Common accused, common witnesses and overlapping evidence created real risks: multiplicity of proceedings, conflicting findings, and security concerns (para 9).</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Precedent followed</strong> — The Court's own order of 4 June 2026 in NIA v. Rahul @ Faisal Karim Masud had transferred West Bengal cases to the same Special Court on like reasoning (para 10).</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Objection distinguished</strong> — Khimji Bhai Jadeja concerned offences in the same series of transactions; a larger-conspiracy allegation stands on a different footing, and clubbing was held permissible (para 11). The petitions were allowed (para 12).</div>
</div>

<h2>Why consolidation matters in conspiracy trials</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Fragmented trials</th><th>Consolidated trial</th></tr>
<tr><td>The same witnesses testify in three courts, with inconsistency risks</td><td>Evidence recorded once before one Special Court</td></tr>
<tr><td>Possibility of conflicting findings on the same conspiracy</td><td>One record, one set of findings</td></tr>
<tr><td>High-risk prisoner movement across States for every hearing</td><td>Reduced movement and security exposure</td></tr>
<tr><td>Parallel bail and interim orders in different forums</td><td>A single court seized of all interlocutory questions</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="check">
<p><strong>For practitioners:</strong> transfer and clubbing petitions of this kind turn on the documentary architecture — the Section 6(5) takeover orders, the MHA's connected-offence determinations, and a concrete showing of common accused and witnesses. For the defence, the battleground that remains is at trial: consolidation does not dilute the prosecution's burden to prove each accused's role in each offence, and severance arguments can still be pressed where prejudice from a joint trial is shown.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>The judgment also clarifies the reach of Khimji Bhai Jadeja: its discipline about same-transaction joinder does not fetter the Supreme Court's power to consolidate cases alleged to be parts of a single larger conspiracy. Forum questions settled, the Lal Qila blast prosecution now proceeds as one trial.</p>
</div>
